= Module 5 — The Internet: Connectivity, Services, Collaboration and Remote Work
**Weeks 10–12 · Lessons 36–41**
**Category of action:** Describing the history and growth of the internet; identifying connectivity technologies and browser features; using social networks and collaborative environments responsibly; using internet services (e-mail, information retrieval); explaining teleworking and videoconferencing, URLs and cookies; setting up a teleconferencing platform.
**Context:** Learners move from how the internet arose (36), how we connect and browse (37), how we collaborate (38), how we use e-mail and search well (39), to working and meeting remotely (40), ending with an integration activity (41).

# Lesson 36: The Internet — History, Growth, Advantages and Disadvantages

> OBJ:
  - Define the internet and outline its history and growth.
  - Explain the advantages and disadvantages of the internet.
  - Describe the impact of the internet on society (education, business, communication).

## Lesson outline (1 period, 50 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 4 min | "What did people do before the internet to…?" (send a letter, find a fact, buy a ticket). | Compare. |
| Core 1 | 16 min | Definition (global network of networks using TCP/IP); history timeline: 1969 ARPANET (UCLA–Stanford), 1971 e-mail (Ray Tomlinson), 1974/1983 TCP/IP (Cerf & Kahn; flag day), 1984 DNS, 1989–91 World Wide Web (Tim Berners-Lee at CERN; HTML/HTTP/URL), 1993 Mosaic browser, 1998 Google, 2004 Facebook/Web 2.0, 2007 iPhone/mobile internet, 2010s cloud & streaming, 2020s 5G, IoT, AI; growth figures (1995 16 million users → 2025 ≈ 5.5 billion, ~68 % of humanity; Cameroon ≈ 40–45 % penetration, mobile-first); Cameroon milestones (1990s Camnet, SAT-3 2002, WACS/ACE 2012–2017, 4G 2015, Camtel fibre, digital-ID projects); governance (ICANN, IETF, W3C, ISOC; ART/ANTIC in Cameroon). | Timeline sheet. |
| Core 2 | 14 min | Advantages (information access, communication, education, commerce, services, entertainment, remote work, innovation, civic participation) vs disadvantages (misinformation, privacy loss, cybercrime, addiction, cyberbullying, inappropriate content, digital divide, cultural homogenisation, environmental cost, job displacement); balanced evaluation. | T-chart. |
| Core 3 | 12 min | Impact on society: **education** (MOOCs, e-learning, OER, Cameroon GCE/Concours info, YouTube tutorials; but inequality), **business** (e-commerce, MoMo, digital marketing, gig work, start-ups – Jumia, Kiro'o Games, Diool; remote services), **communication** (e-mail, WhatsApp, social media, diaspora ties, citizen journalism; misinformation); health, government, culture. | Impact matrix. |
| Conclusion | 4 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 36.1 Definition, history and growth

> DEF:
  The **internet** is the **global "network of networks"** – millions of private, public, academic, business and government networks interconnected through **TCP/IP** protocols, routers and high-capacity links, allowing any connected device to exchange information with any other. The **World Wide Web** is one service on the internet (pages linked by hyperlinks, accessed via HTTP with browsers) – alongside e-mail, file transfer, messaging, streaming and more.

| Year | Milestone |
| 1957–62 | Sputnik → US ARPA; Licklider's "Galactic Network"; packet-switching ideas (Baran, Davies, Kleinrock) |
| **1969** | **ARPANET** – first four nodes (UCLA, SRI, UCSB, Utah); first message "LO" |
| 1971 | **E-mail** (Ray Tomlinson, the @ sign) |
| 1973–74 | Cerf & Kahn design **TCP/IP**; the word "internet" |
| 1983 | ARPANET switches to TCP/IP (1 January – "flag day"); **DNS** (1984) |
| 1989–91 | **Tim Berners-Lee** invents the **World Wide Web** at CERN (HTML, HTTP, URL, first browser/server) |
| 1993 | Mosaic graphical browser; web goes public; NSFNET opens to commerce |
| 1995–98 | Amazon, eBay, Yahoo!, **Google**; Windows 95 with TCP/IP; dial-up boom |
| 2001–07 | Wikipedia, broadband, Skype, Facebook (2004), YouTube (2005), **iPhone (2007)** – mobile internet; Web 2.0 |
| 2008–15 | Android, cloud computing, 4G, WhatsApp, streaming (Netflix), social media dominance |
| 2016–25 | 5G, IoT, video conferencing boom (COVID-19), LEO satellites, generative AI; ≈ **5.5 billion users** (~68 %) |
| **Cameroon** | 1990s: first ISPs/Camnet; **2002 SAT-3** cable at Douala; 2012 WACS (Limbe), 2017 ACE & SAIL (Kribi); 3G 2014, **4G 2015**; Camtel Blue FTTH; mobile money boom; internet penetration ≈ 40–45 %; ANTIC (cybersecurity), ART (regulation), .cm domain |

Growth drivers: cheaper devices (smartphones), mobile networks, submarine fibre, open standards, useful services (search, social, payments), falling data prices. Governance: **ICANN** (names/numbers), **IETF** (protocols), **W3C** (web standards), **ISOC**, regional registries (AFRINIC for Africa).

### 36.2 Advantages and disadvantages

| Advantages | Disadvantages |
| **Information access** – encyclopaedic knowledge, news, research, open data | **Misinformation/fake news**, information overload, unreliable sources |
| **Communication** – instant, cheap, global (e-mail, chat, video calls) | **Privacy loss** – tracking, data harvesting, surveillance |
| **Education** – e-learning, MOOCs, tutorials, OER, remote classes | **Digital divide** – unequal access (rural, cost, gender), widening inequality |
| **Commerce & finance** – e-commerce, online banking, mobile money, global markets for small producers | **Cybercrime** – scams, phishing, fraud, identity theft, ransomware |
| **Services** – e-government, e-health, e-ticketing, job search | **Addiction & health** – screen time, sleep loss, sedentary habits |
| **Entertainment & culture** – streaming, gaming, creativity platforms | **Cyberbullying & harassment**; inappropriate/violent content |
| **Work & innovation** – remote work, freelancing, cloud tools, start-ups | **Job displacement** and precarious gig work |
| **Civic participation** – awareness, petitions, transparency | Manipulation, hate speech, polarisation; **cultural homogenisation** |
| **Efficiency & cost savings** for organisations | **Dependence & outages**; **environmental cost** (energy, e-waste) |

### 36.3 Impact on society

| Domain | Positive impact | Challenges | Cameroon context |
| **Education** | Access to courses (Coursera, Khan Academy, YouTube), digital libraries, virtual labs, teacher collaboration, remote exams info, AI tutors | Unequal access, distraction, plagiarism, quality control | MINESEC/MINESUP portals; e-learning during 2020 school closures; university MOOC pilots; cybercafés as study hubs; the gap between urban and rural schools |
| **Business** | E-commerce (Jumia, Glotelho), digital payments (MTN MoMo, Orange Money), online marketing (Facebook/WhatsApp Business), cloud tools for SMEs, freelancing (Upwork), start-ups (Kiro'o Games, Diool, Njorku) | Fraud, taxation, logistics, competition from global platforms, connectivity costs | Mobile money > bank accounts; informal traders selling via WhatsApp; ANTIC's cybersecurity role |
| **Communication** | Family/diaspora ties (WhatsApp calls), instant news, social movements, citizen journalism, community groups | Misinformation, hate speech, shutdowns, surveillance, loss of face-to-face skills | Diaspora remittances coordinated online; 2017 internet shutdown in NW/SW regions and its economic effect |
| Health | Telemedicine, health information, appointment systems, DHIS2 data | Self-diagnosis errors, data privacy | Mobile health campaigns (vaccination reminders) |
| Government | E-services, transparency, online procedures (visas, taxes) | Digital exclusion, security | Impots.cm online tax filing, e-visa, digital ID projects |
| Culture & entertainment | Music/film distribution (Cameroonian artists on YouTube/Spotify), gaming, languages online | Piracy, dominance of foreign content | Makossa/bikutsi streaming; local content creators |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Three generations, one village near Bafoussam.** Grandmother remembers walking to the post office to send a telegram; her son built a plantain business in 2012 using SMS orders and now runs it through WhatsApp Business and MoMo; his daughter attends a virtual coding club, sells crafts on Instagram and video-calls cousins in Belgium. The same family also lost 80,000 XAF to a phishing SMS and worries about the granddaughter's screen time – advantages and disadvantages coexist.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 36.1 – Timeline ribbon** from ARPANET (1969) to AI era (2025) with icons; a parallel Cameroon lane (SAT-3, WACS, 4G, MoMo). **Figure 36.2 – Growth curve** of internet users worldwide and in Cameroon (log or linear), highlighting the smartphone inflection. **Figure 36.3 – Balance scale** infographic weighing advantages vs disadvantages. **Figure 36.4 – Impact web**: internet at the centre with spokes to education, business, communication, health, government, culture, each with a plus and minus card.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "The internet and the Web are the same." The Web is one application layer service; e-mail, WhatsApp, online games and IoT use the internet without the Web. "The internet was invented by one person/company." It emerged from decades of collaborative research and open standards.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Internet story and debate (35 min).** (1) Timeline build: groups order 15 event cards (world + Cameroon) and add one event they research quickly. (2) Interview homework shared: what a parent/grandparent did before the internet for three tasks. (3) Structured debate: "The internet has done more good than harm for Cameroonian youth" – two teams, evidence required per claim. (4) Impact matrix: each group completes one domain with a local example and presents in 1 minute.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Define the internet and distinguish it from the World Wide Web.
  2. Outline five milestones in the history of the internet with dates, and two milestones for Cameroon.
  3. Explain three factors behind the internet's growth.
  4. Give five advantages and five disadvantages of the internet.
  5. Describe the impact of the internet on education, business and communication, with a Cameroonian example for each.
  6. Which organisations govern internet names, protocols and web standards?
  **Homework:** Write a 300-word reflective essay "How the internet changed my community" including at least three advantages, two disadvantages and one proposal to reduce the digital divide.

