# Lesson 37: Internet Connectivity Technologies and Web Browsers

> OBJ:
  - Identify and describe different technologies used to connect to the internet (dial-up, DSL, fibre, satellite, mobile networks…).
  - Compare internet connection technologies in terms of speed, cost, availability and reliability.
  - Identify and use the features of a web browser (tabs, bookmarks, history, extensions, privacy settings…).

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | "How does this lab reach the internet? How does your phone? Grandparents' village?" | Share. |
| Core 1 | 25 min | Connectivity technologies: **dial-up** (56 kbps over phone line; legacy), **DSL** (ADSL/VDSL over copper; distance-limited), **cable** (DOCSIS; rare in Cameroon), **fibre** (FTTH/FTTB via GPON – Camtel Blue, Orange/MTN fibre), **mobile networks** (2G/3G/4G/5G; dongles/MiFi, tethering; the dominant access in Cameroon), **fixed wireless** (WiMAX legacy, LTE/5G FWA, ISP microwave), **satellite** (VSAT GEO, LEO Starlink), public Wi-Fi/hotspots, leased lines/MPLS for businesses; ISP roles, modems/routers, IP allocation. | Technology cards. |
| Core 2 | 25 min | Comparison table: speed, latency, cost (installation, monthly – XAF), availability (urban/rural), reliability, mobility, suitability; measuring with speed tests; choosing for scenarios. | Comparison + speed test. |
| Core 3 | 35 min | Web browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, Opera, Brave; mobile) – features and hands-on: address/omnibox, **tabs** (pin, group, restore), **bookmarks/favourites** (folders, bar, export), **history** (search, clear), downloads manager, **extensions/add-ons** (ad blockers, password managers, translators; risks), **privacy settings** (cookies, tracking protection, incognito/private mode, clearing data, site permissions – camera/mic/location, HTTPS-only mode, safe browsing, password manager, sync), reader mode, find in page, zoom, print to PDF, developer tools, keyboard shortcuts, default search engine, profiles, accessibility. Step sheet. | Guided browser tour and tasks. |
| Conclusion | 10 min | Exit ticket + reflection. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 37.1 Internet connection technologies

> KEY:
  An **Internet Service Provider (ISP)** connects your home, school or phone to the internet through an **access technology** – wired (dial-up, DSL, cable, fibre) or wireless (mobile networks, fixed wireless, satellite). The right choice depends on where you are, what you need and what you can pay.

| Technology | How it works | Typical speed | Latency | Cost (Cameroon, indicative) | Availability | Reliability | Notes |
| **Dial-up** | Modem converts data to audio tones over a voice phone line; occupies the line | ≤ 56 kbps | High | Very low equipment; per-minute call charges | Wherever a landline exists (rare now) | Poor (drops) | Obsolete; historical |
| **DSL (ADSL/VDSL)** | Data on high frequencies of the copper phone line; always-on; splitter/filter; speed falls with distance from exchange | ADSL 2–24 Mbps down; VDSL up to 100 Mbps | 10–40 ms | Moderate | Urban areas with copper (Camtel legacy) | Moderate (line quality) | Being replaced by fibre |
| **Cable (DOCSIS)** | Data over coaxial TV cable, shared neighbourhood segment | 50 Mbps–1 Gbps | 10–30 ms | Moderate | Limited in Cameroon | Good; slows at peak | Common in Europe/US |
| **Fibre (FTTH/FTTB)** | Light over optical fibre to the building; GPON ONT + router | 20 Mbps–1 Gbps+ symmetrical possible | 2–10 ms | Installation 25,000–100,000 XAF; 20–50k XAF/month (Camtel Blue, MTN/Orange fibre offers) | Cities, main streets, new estates | Excellent (immune to interference, cuts by road works) | Best for schools/offices/heavy homes |
| **Mobile broadband (3G/4G/5G)** | Cellular radio via phone, dongle, MiFi or router with SIM | 3G 1–10 Mbps; **4G 10–100 Mbps**; 5G 100 Mbps–1 Gbps | 30–60 ms (4G), 10–20 ms (5G) | Pay-as-you-go data bundles (e.g. 500–20,000 XAF); router 20–60k XAF | **Widest in Cameroon** (4G in towns and along roads; 3G/2G rural) | Variable (congestion, coverage, power cuts at towers) | Dominant access method; tethering |
| **Fixed wireless access (FWA)** | ISP antenna/microwave or LTE/5G router at a fixed location; line of sight for microwave | 5–500 Mbps | 10–50 ms | Installation + monthly; often for businesses | Towns; hills need LOS | Good; weather-sensitive at high bands | Local ISPs (e.g. Creolink, Yoomee legacy) |
| **Satellite – GEO (VSAT)** | Dish to geostationary satellite 36,000 km | 2–50 Mbps | 500–700 ms | High (dish 300k–1M XAF; expensive monthly) | Anywhere with sky view | Rain fade; otherwise steady | Remote sites, banks, NGOs |
| **Satellite – LEO (Starlink & similar)** | Phased-array dish to low-orbit constellation | 50–250 Mbps | 20–50 ms | Kit ~ 300–400k XAF; ~ 30–60k XAF/month | Anywhere licensed/sky view | Good; power hungry | Rural game-changer where licensed |
| **Public Wi-Fi / hotspots** | Access via a venue's connection (café, campus, "Wi-Fi zones") | Varies | Varies | Free/low | Cities | Variable; security risk | Use VPN/HTTPS |
| **Leased line / MPLS / dedicated fibre** | Guaranteed dedicated bandwidth with SLA | 10 Mbps–10 Gbps symmetric | Low | High | Business districts | Excellent with SLA | Banks, universities, data centres |

### 37.2 Comparing and choosing

| Scenario | Best fit | Reason |
| Urban school with 60 PCs and e-learning | **Fibre** (with 4G backup) | Speed, reliability, cost per Mbps |
| Rural school 30 km from any fibre | **4G FWA** if coverage; else **LEO satellite**; VSAT as last resort | Availability |
| Student at home with one laptop and a phone | **4G bundle/tethering** or entry fibre | Cost, flexibility |
| NGO clinic in a remote area | **LEO satellite** + solar power | Anywhere coverage, latency OK for video |
| Bank branch needing guaranteed uptime | **Leased line/fibre + VSAT or 4G backup** | SLA and redundancy |
| Traveller/field worker | **Mobile broadband** | Mobility |

Measure before deciding: run speed tests (download/upload Mbps, latency ms, jitter) at different times; ask neighbours; check ISP coverage maps; compute cost per GB or per Mbps; consider power (fibre ONTs and satellite kits need electricity/UPS).

### 37.3 Web browser features

> DEF:
  A **web browser** is application software that **requests, receives, renders and lets you interact with web resources** (HTML pages, images, video, web apps) via HTTP/HTTPS – e.g. Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Microsoft Edge, Apple Safari, Opera, Brave; mobile equivalents. Its **rendering engine** (Blink, Gecko, WebKit) turns HTML/CSS/JavaScript into the page you see.

| Feature | What it does | How to use (Chrome/Firefox/Edge) | Tips & risks |
| **Address bar / omnibox** | Enter URLs or search terms; shows padlock (HTTPS) and site info | Type and Enter; click padlock for certificate/permissions | Check the domain carefully (phishing look-alikes) |
| **Tabs** | Multiple pages in one window; pin, group, mute, reopen closed | Ctrl+T new, Ctrl+W close, Ctrl+Shift+T reopen, Ctrl+Tab switch, right-click ▸ Pin/Add to group | Too many tabs slow RAM; use tab groups/search |
| **Bookmarks / favourites** | Save pages for later; folders; bookmarks bar; import/export HTML | Ctrl+D; Ctrl+Shift+B toggle bar; Bookmark manager (Ctrl+Shift+O) | Organise by subject; export as backup |
| **History** | List of visited pages by date; search; clear | Ctrl+H; Ctrl+Shift+Del to clear | Shared computers – clear or use private mode |
| **Downloads manager** | Tracks files; open/show in folder; pause/resume | Ctrl+J | Scan downloads; beware .exe from unknown sites |
| **Extensions / add-ons** | Small programs adding features – ad/tracker blockers (uBlock Origin), password managers (Bitwarden), translators, screenshot tools, reader/accessibility | Chrome Web Store / Firefox Add-ons; Manage extensions | Extensions see your browsing – install only trusted ones; review permissions; remove unused |
| **Private / incognito mode** | No history, cookies or form data kept after closing | Ctrl+Shift+N (Chrome/Edge), Ctrl+Shift+P (Firefox) | Does **not** hide you from ISP/school/websites; not a VPN |
| **Privacy & security settings** | Cookie controls (block third-party), tracking protection, Do Not Track/GPC, clear data on exit, **site permissions** (camera, microphone, location, notifications, pop-ups), safe browsing/phishing warnings, HTTPS-only mode, password manager and breach alerts, sync controls | Settings ▸ Privacy and security; Firefox Enhanced Tracking Protection Strict | Review permissions regularly; block notifications spam; use strong unique passwords with the manager or a dedicated one |
| **Search engine choice** | Default engine (Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo, Ecosia) | Settings ▸ Search engine | Privacy-focused options exist |
| **Reader mode / zoom / find** | Distraction-free reading; Ctrl +/– zoom; Ctrl+F find in page | Toolbar icons | Accessibility |
| **Print / save as PDF / screenshots** | Keep copies of pages | Ctrl+P ▸ Save as PDF; Ctrl+S | Citations for schoolwork |
| **Developer tools** | Inspect HTML/CSS, network requests, console | F12 / Ctrl+Shift+I | Useful for Lesson 46 HTML |
| **Profiles & sync** | Separate work/personal data; sync bookmarks across devices | Profile icon | Sign out on shared PCs |
| **Autofill & passwords** | Saves addresses, cards, passwords | Settings ▸ Autofill | Never save on shared PCs |
| **Pop-up blocker, download protection, sandboxing** | Built-in security | On by default | Keep the browser **updated** |

**Step sheet – "Set up a safe, organised browser" (Chrome/Edge, adaptable):** 1. Update: Menu ▸ Help ▸ About – install updates. 2. Create a profile with your name (shared lab). 3. Settings ▸ Privacy and security ▸ Third-party cookies ▸ Block; Security ▸ Enhanced protection; Site settings ▸ Notifications ▸ Don't allow; Camera/Microphone ▸ Ask. 4. Enable "Always use secure connections". 5. Set default search engine. 6. Install one trusted extension (uBlock Origin) and review its permissions. 7. Create bookmark folders "ICT", "GCE", "Careers"; bookmark three sites; show the bookmarks bar. 8. Open five tabs, pin one, group two, close and reopen one. 9. Open History, search for a site, delete one entry; open a private window and explain what it does/does not hide. 10. Print a page to PDF into your folder.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Choosing a connection for a home in Bertoua.** Options: no DSL on the street; fibre 800 m away (not yet); 4G strong (speed test 35/12 Mbps, 45 ms) with a MiFi at 30,000 XAF and 20 GB for 10,000 XAF; Starlink kit 350,000 XAF (too expensive for now). Decision: 4G router with an external antenna, migrate to fibre when it arrives. On the family laptop the learner sets up separate browser profiles, blocks third-party cookies, installs an ad blocker to save data, bookmarks her revision sites and uses private mode only on the cybercafé PC – knowing it does not hide her from the café's network.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 37.1 – Access technologies map**: a town with fibre along main roads, 4G towers, an FWA mast on a hill, a VSAT dish at a bank, a LEO dish on a rural clinic, DSL from an old exchange – each labelled with speed/latency/cost badges. **Figure 37.2 – Comparison radar chart** (speed, latency, cost, availability, reliability, mobility) for fibre, 4G, satellite, DSL. **Figure 37.3 – Annotated browser window**: omnibox with padlock, tabs (pinned/grouped), bookmarks bar, menu, extension icons, profile, downloads, and the Privacy settings panel with key toggles. **Figure 37.4 – Private-mode myth-buster** showing what is and isn't hidden.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Incognito makes me anonymous." It only stops local history/cookies; the ISP, school network, employer and sites still see you. "Faster Mbps always means better." Latency and reliability matter more for video calls; satellite GEO has decent Mbps but 600 ms delay.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Connect and browse (55 min, lab).** (1) Speed-test the lab connection and a phone hotspot; record download/upload/latency; identify the access technology of each (ISP name, device). (2) Connectivity clinic: for six scenarios recommend a technology with cost estimates from ISP price lists (research online). (3) Complete the browser step sheet; screenshot evidence of: blocked third-party cookies, bookmark folders, a tab group, cleared history item, site permission settings, an extension's permissions. (4) Privacy audit: check which sites have camera/mic/location permissions on a lab browser and revoke unnecessary ones. (5) No-computer alternative: paper browser mock-up labelling features; card sort of connection technologies by speed/cost/availability.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Identify and describe six technologies used to connect to the internet.
  2. Compare fibre, 4G mobile broadband, DSL and satellite (GEO and LEO) in terms of speed, latency, cost, availability and reliability.
  3. Recommend a connection for: an urban school; a rural clinic; a travelling salesperson – justify.
  4. Name eight browser features and explain the purpose of each.
  5. Explain what private/incognito mode does and does not do.
  6. List five privacy settings you should configure in a browser and why extensions can be risky.
  **Homework:** Survey five households/businesses about their internet connection (technology, ISP, speed test, monthly cost, problems); tabulate and recommend improvements; also write a one-page browser safety guide for younger students.

