# Lesson 38: Social Networks and Collaborative Environments

> OBJ:
  - Describe social networking platforms and their features.
  - Explain the benefits and risks of social networks.
  - Use collaborative environments and tools (e.g., Google Workspace, Microsoft Teams, shared documents) for group work.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room/phones)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Poll: platforms used, hours per day; one good and one bad experience. | Respond. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | Social networking platforms – definition, categories (general: Facebook, X, Instagram, TikTok, Snapchat; messaging: WhatsApp, Telegram; professional: LinkedIn; video: YouTube; discussion: Reddit, Discord; local groups), common features (profiles, connections/followers, feeds & algorithms, posts/stories/reels, likes/comments/shares, messaging, groups/pages/communities, live streaming, hashtags, notifications, marketplace, ads, privacy controls, verification), business models (attention & data → ads). | Feature matrix. |
| Core 2 | 25 min | Benefits (connection, information, learning communities, business/marketing, activism, support, creativity, employment) vs risks (privacy/data harvesting, misinformation, cyberbullying, scams/impersonation, addiction/algorithmic design, mental health, oversharing/digital footprint, predators, hate speech, legal consequences – Cameroon's 2010 cybercrime law); safe-use practices (privacy settings, 2FA, think-before-posting, verify sources, report/block, digital footprint audit). | Risk-mitigation table; privacy check on own account. |
| Core 3 | 40 min | Collaborative environments: cloud suites (**Google Workspace** – Docs/Sheets/Slides/Drive/Meet/Chat/Classroom; **Microsoft 365** – Word/Excel/PowerPoint online, OneDrive, **Teams**, SharePoint), project tools (Trello, Notion, Asana), communication (Slack/Discord/WhatsApp groups), wikis/shared notes (Wikipedia model, OneNote), version control (GitHub) mention; features: **real-time co-editing**, comments & suggestions, sharing permissions (viewer/commenter/editor; link vs people), version history, chat/video, task assignment, notifications, offline mode. Hands-on: shared document workflow. | Guided collaborative task. |
| Conclusion | 10 min | Reflection and exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 38.1 Social networking platforms and their features

> DEF:
  A **social networking platform** is an online service that lets users **create profiles, connect with others and share content** (text, images, video, live streams) within networks of friends, followers or communities. **Collaborative environments** are online spaces and tools where groups **work together on shared documents, projects and communication** – often in real time, from different places.

| Category | Examples | Typical use |
| General social | Facebook, Instagram, X (Twitter), TikTok, Snapchat, Threads | Sharing life updates, news, entertainment |
| Messaging-centred | **WhatsApp** (dominant in Cameroon), Telegram, Signal, Messenger | Groups, voice notes, business chats |
| Professional | LinkedIn | Careers, networking, recruitment |
| Video/creator | YouTube, TikTok, Twitch | Learning, entertainment, monetised content |
| Discussion/community | Reddit, Discord, Quora, Facebook Groups | Interest communities, support |
| Local/niche | Cameroonian Facebook groups (job boards, "Douala Astuces"), alumni WhatsApp groups | Community information |

| Feature | Description | Note |
| Profile | Name, photo, bio, contact – your public identity | Control what is visible |
| Connections | Friends (mutual), followers (one-way), contacts | Accepting strangers = risk |
| Feed & algorithm | Stream of posts ranked by engagement prediction | Designed to maximise time spent |
| Posts, stories, reels/shorts, live | Content formats: permanent, 24-hour, short video, live streaming | Live/stories still get screenshotted |
| Reactions, comments, shares/retweets | Engagement; virality | Sharing spreads misinformation fast |
| Direct messaging | Private chats, voice/video calls | Scams often start in DMs |
| Groups, pages, channels, communities | Topic or organisation spaces; admins/moderators | Useful for classes and clubs |
| Hashtags, mentions, tagging | Discovery and notification | Tagging others reveals location/links |
| Notifications | Push alerts | Manage to reduce addiction |
| Marketplace/shops, ads, promotions | Commerce; targeted advertising based on your data | Business model of "free" platforms |
| Privacy & safety settings | Audience selectors, blocking, reporting, 2FA, activity/download data, ad preferences | Review termly |
| Verification badges | Authenticity of accounts (now sometimes paid) | Not proof of truth |

### 38.2 Benefits and risks

| Benefits | Risks | Safe practices |
| **Connection** with family, friends, diaspora; support networks | **Privacy loss** – oversharing, data harvesting, location leaks | Set posts to friends-only; disable location; audit apps connected to your account |
| **Information & awareness** – news, alerts, opportunities (scholarships, jobs) | **Misinformation/fake news**, deepfakes, rumours (e.g. false exam-date posts) | Verify with official sources; check dates/authors; reverse-image search |
| **Learning communities** – study groups, tutorials, expert Q&A | **Distraction & addiction** – endless feeds, notifications; sleep loss | Time limits (Digital Wellbeing/Screen Time), notification control |
| **Business & marketing** – WhatsApp Business, Facebook shops, influencer income | **Scams & fraud** – fake sellers, "MoMo double" scams, phishing links, impersonation | Never pay strangers in advance; check profiles; report impersonation |
| **Activism & civic voice** | **Cyberbullying, harassment, hate speech** | Block/report; keep evidence; tell an adult; laws (Cameroon Law 2010/012 on cybersecurity and cybercrime punishes online harassment/defamation) |
| **Creativity & self-expression** | **Mental health** – comparison, FOMO, body image | Curate feeds; follow positive accounts; breaks |
| **Employment** – LinkedIn networking, portfolio | **Digital footprint** – posts seen by schools/employers years later | "Grandmother test" before posting; review old posts |
| **Emergency communication** | **Predators & grooming**, sextortion | Never share intimate images; report; privacy settings for minors |
| Cheap communication (data vs airtime) | **Account hacking** | Strong unique password + 2FA; beware login links |

### 38.3 Collaborative environments and tools

| Tool / suite | Components | Collaboration features | Best for |
| **Google Workspace** (free with Gmail; Education edition for schools) | Docs, Sheets, Slides, Forms, Drive, Meet, Chat, Calendar, Classroom, Keep, Sites | Real-time co-editing with coloured cursors; comments and @mentions; **Suggesting mode**; **version history** with named versions; share with people or link (Viewer/Commenter/Editor); Drive shared folders; Meet video; Classroom assignments; offline mode | Schools, group projects, light document work |
| **Microsoft 365** | Word/Excel/PowerPoint (desktop + online), OneDrive, **Teams**, SharePoint, OneNote, Forms, Planner, Outlook | Co-authoring in Office files; comments/track changes; Teams channels, chat, meetings, file tabs, assignments (Teams for Education); SharePoint sites; Planner boards | Organisations already on Office; schools with Microsoft licences |
| Messaging/communities | WhatsApp groups/communities, Telegram, Slack, Discord | Channels, threads, file sharing, calls, polls, pinned messages | Quick coordination; class groups |
| Project/task management | Trello, Asana, Notion, Planner, ClickUp | Kanban boards, tasks, due dates, assignments, checklists | Organising a group project |
| Shared notes & wikis | OneNote, Notion, Google Keep, MediaWiki, Confluence | Shared notebooks, linked pages, history | Knowledge bases, study notes |
| Design & whiteboards | Canva (teams), Figma, Miro, Jamboard alternatives | Simultaneous design, sticky notes | Posters, brainstorming |
| Code | GitHub/GitLab | Version control, issues, pull requests | Programming projects |
| Video meetings | Google Meet, Teams, Zoom, Jitsi Meet (free/open source) | Screen share, recording, breakout rooms, chat | Remote classes (Lesson 40) |

**Good practice for group work:** agree tools and naming conventions; one shared folder with clear ownership; use Suggesting/track changes for reviews; @mention for specific requests; comment threads resolved when done; version history instead of "final_v3_REAL.docx"; assign tasks with deadlines on a board; short check-in meetings; respect data/time (compressed images, async messages); permissions – Editor only for members, Viewer for teachers unless required; export a PDF at each milestone; keep an offline copy.

**Step sheet – shared document workflow (Google Docs; Microsoft Word Online similar):** 1. Leader creates "Group 3 – E-services report" in a shared Drive folder. 2. Share ▸ add members as **Editor**, teacher as **Commenter**; copy link with "Restricted". 3. Members open simultaneously; each writes their section (observe coloured cursors). 4. Reviewer switches to **Suggesting**; author accepts/rejects. 5. Insert comments with @name for questions; resolve when addressed. 6. File ▸ Version history ▸ Name current version "Draft 1"; view a previous version and restore a paragraph. 7. Insert a chart from a shared Sheet; Slides linked. 8. Add tasks to a Trello/Planner board with owners and dates. 9. Hold a 10-minute Meet check-in with screen share. 10. Download as PDF and submit via Classroom/Teams.

> EXAMPLE:
  **A Yaoundé students' association goes digital.** Their WhatsApp community (announcements channel + subgroups) replaced 40 phone calls per event; membership data lives in a shared Google Sheet with Editor rights for two secretaries only; flyers are co-designed in Canva; the annual report is written by five members in one Google Doc with Suggesting mode and named versions; meetings with alumni in France run on Meet. After a fake "fundraising" page impersonated them, they verified their Facebook page, enabled 2FA for all admins and published official contact channels – benefits harvested, risks managed.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 38.1 – Anatomy of a social platform**: profile card, feed with algorithm "engine", post types, reactions, DMs, groups, ads – with privacy toggles highlighted. **Figure 38.2 – Benefit/risk see-saw** with safe-practice weights. **Figure 38.3 – Collaborative workspace screenshot mock-up**: Google Doc with three coloured cursors, a comment thread, Suggesting mode change, sharing dialog (Editor/Commenter/Viewer), version history panel; a Teams channel beside it. **Figure 38.4 – Group-work workflow**: plan (board) → create (shared docs) → review (suggestions/comments) → meet (video) → publish (PDF).

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Deleted posts are gone." Screenshots, caches and platform backups persist – assume permanence. "Sharing the link with 'Anyone with the link – Editor' is fine." Anyone who receives the link (forwarded) can edit or delete; restrict to people and roles.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Collaborate for real (55 min, groups of four, lab/phones).** (1) Privacy audit: each learner reviews one platform's privacy settings (audience, location, 2FA, connected apps) using a checklist and fixes at least three items (screenshots without personal data). (2) Misinformation check: analyse three viral Cameroonian posts (teacher-provided) with a verification checklist. (3) Shared-document sprint: complete the step sheet to co-write a one-page "Safe social media guide for Form 1" – each member a section, one reviewer in Suggesting mode, named versions, task board, 5-minute video check-in, PDF export. (4) Reflection: what worked, conflicts (simultaneous edits), how permissions were set and why. (5) No-computer alternative: "paper Google Doc" – one sheet passed around with coloured pens as cursors and sticky-note comments.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe four categories of social networking platforms with examples and eight common features.
  2. Explain five benefits and five risks of social networks, each with a safe practice.
  3. What is a digital footprint and why does it matter for future employment?
  4. Describe three collaborative tools and the features that support group work (co-editing, comments, permissions, version history).
  5. Explain the difference between Viewer, Commenter and Editor sharing roles and between "restricted" and "anyone with the link".
  6. Outline a workflow for a four-person group producing a shared report.
  **Homework:** Produce, in a shared document with at least two classmates (version history as evidence), a one-page digital-citizenship charter for your class; individually, write a 150-word reflection on your own social-media habits and one change you will make.

