# Lesson 39: Internet Services — E-mail and Information Retrieval

> OBJ:
  - Identify and describe common internet services (e-mail, WWW, FTP, VoIP, instant messaging, cloud services…).
  - Create an e-mail account and send an e-mail with an attachment.
  - Perform effective information retrieval using search engines (keywords, operators, evaluating sources).

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min – computer room)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | "What did you do online this week?" – map to services. | Brainstorm. |
| Core 1 | 20 min | Internet services table: **WWW**, **e-mail**, **FTP/file sharing/cloud storage**, **VoIP/video calls**, **instant messaging**, streaming, social networking, e-commerce/payments, cloud computing (SaaS/PaaS/IaaS), search engines, online gaming, remote access (SSH/RDP), IoT services, newsgroups/forums, e-learning; protocols and examples per service. | Service–protocol matrix. |
| Core 2 | 40 min | E-mail in depth: how e-mail works (client → SMTP → recipient's server → IMAP/POP3 → client; webmail), address anatomy, headers (To/Cc/Bcc/Subject), attachments (size limits ~25 MB; cloud links; formats; compress), etiquette (subject line, greeting, concise, signature, reply vs reply-all, forward), folders/labels/filters, spam/phishing recognition, security (2FA, recovery, HTTPS), professional address for GCE/university applications. **Hands-on step sheet**: create a Gmail/Outlook account (or use lab accounts), set up profile & 2FA, compose and send an e-mail with an attached document and an image to the teacher and Cc a classmate, reply, forward, create a label/folder and a filter, add a signature. | Create account; send e-mail with attachment. |
| Core 3 | 27 min | Information retrieval: search engines (Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo; how indexing/ranking works), choosing **keywords** (specific nouns, synonyms, remove stop words), **operators** (quotes " ", minus –, OR, site:, filetype:, intitle:, define:, .., *; Tools ▸ time range; image/reverse search; Google Scholar), refining queries, **evaluating sources** (CRAAP: Currency, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy, Purpose; domain clues .gov.cm/.edu/.org; cross-checking; bias; AI-generated content caution), citing sources, saving/organising findings. Hands-on search challenge. | Search challenge; source evaluation. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 39.1 Common internet services

| Service | Description | Protocols / technologies | Examples |
| **World Wide Web (WWW)** | Linked hypertext pages and web applications accessed by browsers | HTTP/HTTPS, HTML/CSS/JS, URLs, DNS | News sites, portals, web apps |
| **E-mail** | Store-and-forward electronic messages with attachments to addresses user@domain | SMTP (send), IMAP/POP3 (receive), webmail over HTTPS | Gmail, Outlook.com, Yahoo, institutional mail |
| **File transfer & cloud storage** | Upload/download and synchronise files; sharing links | FTP/SFTP, HTTPS, WebDAV | Google Drive, OneDrive, Dropbox, WeTransfer, FileZilla |
| **VoIP & video calling** | Voice/video over IP instead of circuit telephony | SIP, RTP/SRTP, WebRTC | WhatsApp calls, Zoom, Meet, Teams, Skype |
| **Instant messaging & chat** | Real-time text, voice notes, media, groups | XMPP, proprietary (Signal protocol E2E encryption) | WhatsApp, Telegram, Messenger, Signal |
| **Search engines** | Index and rank web content for queries | Crawlers, HTTPS | Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo |
| **Social networking** | Profiles, sharing, communities (Lesson 38) | HTTPS APIs | Facebook, TikTok, LinkedIn |
| **Streaming media** | On-demand/live audio-video | HTTPS/DASH/HLS, RTMP | YouTube, Netflix, Spotify, Boomplay |
| **E-commerce & online payments** | Buying/selling, digital payments | HTTPS, payment APIs, USSD | Jumia, MoMo/Orange Money, PayPal |
| **Cloud computing** | On-demand computing resources: **SaaS** (apps), **PaaS** (platforms), **IaaS** (servers/storage) | HTTPS, virtualisation | Google Workspace (SaaS), Microsoft 365, AWS/Azure (IaaS) |
| **E-learning** | Courses, LMS, MOOCs | HTTPS, LMS (Moodle, Classroom) | Coursera, Khan Academy, MINESEC e-learning |
| **Remote access** | Control a distant computer/terminal | SSH, RDP, VNC, VPN | IT support, servers |
| **Online gaming** | Multiplayer games, e-sports | UDP/TCP, WebSocket | PUBG Mobile, eFootball |
| **Forums, newsgroups, wikis, blogs** | Discussion and publishing | HTTPS (legacy NNTP) | Reddit, Wikipedia, WordPress |
| **IoT & mapping services** | Connected devices; location services | MQTT, HTTPS, GPS | Google Maps, smart meters |
| **E-government & e-services** | Official procedures online (Lesson 42–43) | HTTPS | Tax filing, e-visa |

### 39.2 E-mail

> DEF:
  **Electronic mail (e-mail)** is a store-and-forward service for exchanging messages and attached files between addresses of the form **username@domain** (e.g. nadia.fotso@gmail.com). Messages travel from the sender's client to their mail server (SMTP), across the internet to the recipient's mail server (SMTP), where they wait until the recipient's client downloads (POP3) or synchronises (IMAP) them – or reads them via webmail.

| Element | Description |
| Address | `username@domain` – local part + @ + mail domain (gmail.com, minesec.gov.cm) |
| **To / Cc / Bcc** | Main recipients / copied (visible) / blind copies (hidden – use for mass mails to protect privacy) |
| **Subject** | Short, specific ("Application for Form 5 Science Club – Nadia Fotso") |
| Body | Greeting, purpose in the first lines, clear paragraphs, courteous close, **signature** (name, class/role, contact) |
| **Attachments** | Files sent with the message; limits (Gmail/Outlook ≈ 25 MB – larger via Drive/OneDrive link); prefer PDF for documents, compressed images; name files clearly; scan for viruses |
| Reply / Reply all / Forward | Reply to sender; reply to everyone (use sparingly); forward to a new recipient (remove sensitive history) |
| Folders/labels, filters, archive, search, stars | Organisation and automation (e.g. label all mail from school) |
| Spam/junk, phishing | Unsolicited or fraudulent mail – check sender address, links (hover), urgency, spelling; never send passwords/PINs; report |
| Security | Strong password, **2-step verification**, recovery phone/e-mail, HTTPS webmail, log out on shared PCs, beware attachments (.exe, macros) |
| Etiquette (netiquette) | Professional address, proper subject, no ALL CAPS, proofread, respond within a reasonable time, respect confidentiality, avoid heavy attachments |
| Webmail vs client | Browser (Gmail.com, Outlook.com) vs app (Outlook, Thunderbird, mobile Mail) configured with IMAP/SMTP |

**Step sheet – create an account and send an e-mail with an attachment (Gmail; Outlook.com similar):** 1. Go to gmail.com ▸ Create account ▸ For my personal use. 2. Enter first/last name, date of birth, gender. 3. Choose a professional username (firstname.lastname; add digits if taken); create a strong password (12+ chars, mixed) and confirm. 4. Add a recovery phone/e-mail; verify code by SMS. 5. Accept terms; skip optional offers. 6. Settings ▸ See all settings ▸ General ▸ Signature: "Nadia Fotso | Upper Sixth Science | Lycée Bilingue de Yaoundé"; Save. 7. Google Account ▸ Security ▸ 2-Step Verification ▸ turn on (phone prompt/SMS). 8. **Compose**: To = teacher's address; Cc = classmate; Subject = "ICT Lesson 39 – Practical submission – Nadia Fotso"; Body = greeting, one paragraph describing the attachment, close, signature. 9. Click the **paper-clip** ▸ select `Lesson39_report.pdf` (< 25 MB) and an image; wait for upload; (for a large file: Drive icon ▸ insert link). 10. Proofread; **Send**; open Sent to confirm. 11. Open the teacher's reply; **Reply** with a thank-you; **Forward** the original to yourself with the history trimmed. 12. Create label "ICT" and a filter (from: teacher → apply label, never spam). 13. Mark a sample phishing mail as spam; report it. 14. Sign out (shared PC).

### 39.3 Effective information retrieval

> KEY:
  A **search engine** crawls the web, indexes pages and ranks results by relevance, authority, freshness and location. Effective retrieval means choosing **precise keywords**, using **operators** to refine, and **evaluating** results critically before using or citing them.

| Technique | How | Example |
| **Keywords** | Use specific nouns and distinctive terms; drop stop words; add context (place, year, format); try synonyms and the language of experts | `cameroon GCE ICT syllabus 2026 pdf` instead of `what is the ict syllabus` |
| **Exact phrase " "** | Forces the phrase | `"transmission media" guided unguided` |
| **Exclude –** | Removes a term | `jaguar speed -car` |
| **OR / \|** | Either term | `telework OR telecommuting cameroon` |
| **site:** | Within a domain/type | `site:minesec.gov.cm calendrier`; `site:.edu machine learning` |
| **filetype:** | Specific files | `filetype:pdf OSI model tutorial` |
| **intitle: / inurl:** | Words in title/URL | `intitle:"lesson plan" networking` |
| **define:** | Definitions | `define:extranet` |
| **Number range ..** | Ranges | `laptop 200000..300000 XAF` |
| **Wildcard *** | Unknown word | `"internet was invented in *"` |
| Tools ▸ time range, language, region | Recency and locale | Past year for prices |
| **Reverse image search** | Verify images | Google Lens / TinEye |
| **Google Scholar, library databases, Wikipedia references** | Academic sources | Scholar: `"digital divide" Cameroon` |
| AI answer engines | Useful summaries; **verify** – can hallucinate; follow citations | – |

| Evaluating a source – CRAAP test | Questions | Red flags |
| **Currency** | When published/updated? Do links work? | Undated; old statistics for fast-changing topics |
| **Relevance** | Does it answer your question at the right level/context (Cameroon)? | Off-topic, foreign context assumed universal |
| **Authority** | Who wrote it? Credentials? Institution? Domain (.gov.cm, .edu, ITU, WHO)? Contact info? | Anonymous, no about page, look-alike domains |
| **Accuracy** | Evidence, references, verifiable facts, consistent with other reputable sources? Spelling/grammar? | No sources, sensational, contradicts consensus |
| **Purpose** | Inform, teach, sell, persuade, entertain? Bias? Ads? Sponsored? | Hidden agenda, clickbait, "miracle" claims |

Also: **cross-check** ≥ 2 independent sources; distinguish primary/secondary sources; note **date accessed**; cite (APA/MLA or a simple format: author, title, site, URL, date); organise with bookmarks/Zotero/notes; respect copyright/licensing (Creative Commons for images).

> EXAMPLE:
  **Researching "impact of Starlink on rural education in Cameroon".** Poor: `starlink cameroon schools good?` → blogs and ads. Better: `"Starlink" Cameroon schools OR education 2024..2026 site:.cm OR site:.org` → ministry/NGO reports; `filetype:pdf "satellite internet" Africa education ITU` → ITU study; Scholar for peer-reviewed papers; reverse-image check on a viral photo of a "Starlink classroom" reveals it was taken in Kenya. CRAAP test rejects a sensational blog (no author, 2021 data, selling kits) and keeps the ITU report and a MINPOSTEL communiqué. The e-mail to the teacher attaches the sourced summary as PDF with a proper subject line.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 39.1 – Internet services wheel** with icons and protocols. **Figure 39.2 – E-mail journey**: sender's client → SMTP server → internet → recipient's server → IMAP/POP3/webmail → recipient; labelled with ports and where TLS protects it. **Figure 39.3 – Annotated compose window**: To/Cc/Bcc, subject, body structure, attachment clip, Drive link, send. **Figure 39.4 – Search operator cheat sheet** and a **CRAAP scorecard** with a sample site scored.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "The first result is the best/true." Ranking mixes relevance, popularity and ads; evaluate. "Bcc is sneaky." It protects recipients' privacy in group mails. "An attachment that is a .zip from a known sender is safe." Accounts get hijacked – verify unexpected attachments.

> ACTIVITY:
  **E-mail and search lab (55 min).** (1) Complete the e-mail step sheet: account (or lab account), signature, 2FA, send an e-mail with a PDF and image attached to the teacher with a classmate in Cc; reply and forward; create a label and filter; identify two phishing signs in a sample. (2) Search challenge (20 min): ten questions requiring operators (e.g. "Find a PDF from a Cameroonian government site about the 2026 school calendar"; "Find the year Camtel's SAT-3 landing was inaugurated with two sources"; "Find a Creative-Commons image of a fibre cable"); record query, operator used, source URL, CRAAP score. (3) Source evaluation: score three given pages with the CRAAP rubric and justify accept/reject. (4) No-computer alternative: paper e-mail form with headers/attachment checklist; operator-matching cards; printed pages for CRAAP scoring.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Identify and describe eight common internet services, naming a protocol and an example for each.
  2. Explain how an e-mail travels from sender to recipient, naming the protocols.
  3. List the steps to create an e-mail account and send a message with an attachment; explain To, Cc and Bcc.
  4. State six rules of e-mail etiquette and four signs of a phishing e-mail.
  5. Write refined search queries using at least four operators for a given research question.
  6. Apply the CRAAP test to evaluate a source; why cross-check?
  **Homework:** Send the teacher an e-mail (proper subject, greeting, signature) attaching a one-page PDF answering a research question of your choice, with three sources evaluated using CRAAP and the exact queries/operators used; Cc a study partner.

